5:## Dissecting heterogeneity in major depressive disorder via normative model-driven subtyping of functional brain networks
28:Major depressive disorder (MDD), a widespread and intricate mental health  condition  affecting  nearly  300  million  people  worldwide  (Cui et al., 2024), is characterized by a wide range of complex symptoms. These include persistent sadness, anhedonia, as well as severe physical and  cognitive  impairments,  which  can  present  in  various  symptom combinations among different individuals (Marx et al., 2023; Milaneschi et al., 2021). A significant challenge in understanding MDD lies in its heterogeneity  that  manifests  as  variations  in  clinical  presentations, treatment responses, and underlying brain mechanisms (Kennis et al., 2020;  Toenders  et  al.,  2020).  Exploring  this  neurophysiological  heterogeneity is crucial for linking complex biological dysregulations to
34:Methods: To address this problem, we explored the brain network topology using resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (rs-fMRI) data from a cohort of 797 MDD patients and 822 matched healthy controls (HC). Utilizing normative modeling of HC, we quantified individual deviations in brain network degree centrality among  MDD  patients.  Through  k-means  clustering  of  these  deviation  profiles,  we  identified  two  clinically meaningful MDD subtypes. Moreover, we employed Neurosynth to analyze the cognitive correlates of these subtypes.
40:clinical manifestations, thereby enhancing the diagnostic accuracy and treatment efficacy for MDD.
66:Normative modeling has emerged as a promising tool to overcome these limitations by quantifying abnormalities at the individual rather than group level (Marquand et al., 2019; Rutherford et al., 2022). This approach constructs normative standards based on large-scale neuroimaging datasets of healthy populations and leverages statistical models to map the expected range of brain function or structure at a particular age, allowing for the identification of deviations in individual patients relative to their corresponding normative models (Remiszewski et al., 2022; Rutherford et al., 2022). Normative modeling has been successfully applied to explore the heterogeneity of various neuropsychiatric disorders.  For  example,  using  normative  modeling,  researchers  have successfully revealed high heterogeneity in anatomical loci of schizophrenia-related changes (Lv et al., 2021). Another study has also found that the brain functional phenotypes and cognitive decline trajectories were greatly heterogeneous in Alzheimer's disease and identified  two  subtypes  based  on  their  individual  deviation  patterns  (Huo et  al.,  2024).  These  studies  have  demonstrated  that  deviations  from normative models can effectively characterize differences between individual  patients  and  healthy  controls,  as  well  as  among  patients themselves,  and  can  be  used  to  identify  potential  neurobiological subtypes.
68:In this study, we aimed to dissect the heterogeneity in MDD through normative model-driven subtyping of functional brain networks. Utilizing rs-fMRI data from a cohort of 797 MDD patients and 822 matched healthy  controls,  we  investigated  individual  deviations  in  functional brain network topology. By applying k-means clustering to deviation profiles derived from normative models of healthy brains, we sought to identify distinct MDD subtypes characterized by their unique functional brain network patterns. Moreover, we employed Neurosynth, a platform for large-scale automated synthesis of fMRI data (Yarkoni et al., 2011), to explore the cognitive correlates of these subtypes, gaining insights into how brain alterations may affect cognition. We hypothesized that MDD patients exhibited heterogeneity in the topological structure of functional brain networks, and there were potential MDD subtypes, each with  distinct  neurobiological  mechanisms,  clinical  symptoms,  and cognitive functions.
82:## 2.3. Capturing individual deviations from normative models
84:To implement normative modeling, we utilized the PCNtoolkit Python  package,  version  0.28  (Rutherford  et  al.,  2022).  Normative modeling  maps  demographic  or  behavioral  variables  onto  neurobiological variables and provides estimates of variation percentiles within the cohort by modeling the relationship between the two (Segal et al., 2023). In this study, age and sex were mapped onto the degree centrality of brain functional networks. By placing individuals within a normative range,  we  analyzed  and  quantified  each  patient's  deviation  patterns
88:Fig. 1. Flowchart illustrating the process of normative modeling and its application in major depressive disorder. (A) Calculation of degree centrality. For each subject, the functional connectivity network was constructed and the area under the curve (AUC) of the network's degree centrality was calculated. (B) Normative model development. Hierarchical Bayesian regression was trained based on the degree centrality of healthy controls to establish a normative model for each region, into which the degree centrality of MDD patients was input. (C) Deviation assessment. The deviations derived from step (B) were used to ascertain the deviation pattern for each MDD patient. (D) Identification of extremely deviated regions. Percentages of patients with extreme deviations in each region were calculated to pinpoint regions where outliers occurred most frequently. (E) Subtype classification and characterization. MDD subtypes were identified through analyzing individual deviation patterns, and their neuroimaging, clinical, and cognitive differences were characterized. MDD, Major depressive disorder; HC, healthy controls; DC, degree centrality.
92:relative  to  the  healthy  population;  concurrently,  we  identified  their abnormal characteristics as outliers from this range (Gaiser et al., 2024; Verdi et al., 2023). To minimize the impact of site-related variability on subsequent results, hierarchical Bayesian regression (HBR) was employed in the HC cohort to develop a normative model of network degree  centrality  as  a  function  of  age  and  sex  for  each  brain  region (Fig. 1B). HBR allows for the imposition of a common prior distribution across multiple sites to estimate site-specific yet interrelated means and variances, thus accommodating signal and noise variability in multisite data while preserving meaningful site-related variations (Bayer et al., 2022; Kia et al., 2020). To evaluate the generalizability of the normative models, we first performed normative modeling on the HC using 10-fold cross-validation,  and  assessed  the  models  using  overall  normalized mean squared error and mean squared log-loss. Specifically, we stratified the entire HC dataset by site and randomly divided it into 10 folds. In each modeling iteration, we used 9 folds to train the models, with the remaining 1 fold serving as the test set to evaluate the models' generalizability. This process was repeated 10 times to ensure that each fold was excluded once. Then, the normative models were trained on all HC data and later applied to MDD patients. To quantify deviations from these normative models in individual brain regions, we employed a zscore metric derived by normalizing the difference between actual and predicted  degree  centrality  based  on  prediction  uncertainty.  Specifically, for each subject i at each brain region j , we calculated the z-score based on the predicted degree centrality ̂ yij , the actual degree centrality yij ,  the  predictive  uncertainty σ ij,  and  the  normative variance σ nj ,  as follows:
96:A  positive  zij indicated  higher  degree  centrality  in  MDD  patients relative to HC, whereas a negative zij signified the opposite. For each MDD patient, the degree centrality values of their brain regions were plotted on normative percentile charts derived from HC data, enabling the estimation of individual deviations (Fig. 1C).
106:After  distinguishing  subtypes,  we  conducted  further  analyses  to compare  their  demographic  profiles  and  clinical  variables  (Fig.  1E). Using a similar approach, we generated spatial overlap maps specific to each  subtype,  portraying  the  percentages  of  individuals  exhibiting positive or negative outliers in various regions. We then compared these subtype-specific maps with the overall spatial overlap maps to investigate  whether  the  distribution  of  outliers  in  brain  regions  was  more consistent  within  MDD  subtypes  than  across  the  entire  MDD  patient population.
124:## 3.1. Individual deviations from normative models
126:The 10-fold cross-validation performed on the HC revealed the high generalizability  of  the  normative  models'  fitting  performance.  The overall standardized mean squared error was close to 1 (1.014 ± 0.005), and  the  mean  squared  log-loss  was  close  to  0  (0.001 ± 0.004),  see Fig.  S2.  The  normative  models  were  then  constructed  using  the  HC group  and  subsequently  applied  to  quantify  individual  deviations  in each brain region across all MDD patients. Fig. 2A and Fig. S3 visually portrayed the spatial distribution of mean deviation patterns across all MDD patients and age-related degree centrality change trajectories in both female and male groups. Specifically, age-related increases in degree centrality were significant in the left lateral occipital cortex and the right frontal orbital cortex, while decreased degree centrality with age were  observed  across  several  regions  including  the  left  superior
134:Fig. 2. Normative models established based on HCs' degree centrality and individual deviations from normative models in MDD patients. (A) The brain map in the center displays the mean deviation pattern for all MDD patients. The models on either side represent age-related trends in degree centrality across various regions within a single site, alongside the corresponding normative ranges (female MDD: red dots; 95th percentiles: blue dashed lines; 99th percentiles: light blue dashed lines). (B) Cortical maps and brain slice images of spatial overlap quantify the proportion of patients exhibiting positive (left) and negative (right) outliers in each region. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the web version of this article.)
164:In  this  study,  we  quantitatively  evaluated  the  heterogeneity  of functional brain networks in MDD patients. Our analysis revealed intersubject  differences  in  the  spatial  distribution  and  directional  characteristics of individual deviations, underscoring the intricate complexity of MDD neuropathology. Moreover, we identified two distinct neurobiological subtypes of MDD, each distinguished by its unique topological abnormalities, clinical presentations, and cognitive profiles. These discoveries  offered  profound insights into the heterogeneity inherent in MDD,  advancing  our  understanding  of its subclassification and,
184:Fig.  4. Differences in clinical variables between MDD subtypes. (A) Subtype differences in onset age and scores on the HAMD-17 subscales (General Somatic Symptoms and Hypochondriasis), * p &lt; 0.05. (B) The correlations between deviation values of the right inferior temporal gyrus (posterior division) and LIM with HAMD-17 total scores in each subtype. HAMD-17: 17-item Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression; ITGpost.R: right inferior temporal gyrus (posterior division); LIM, limbic network.
190:Utilizing normative models based on the network degree centrality from a healthy reference cohort, we measured the deviation patterns for individual MDD patients and elucidated the spatial distribution of deviation outliers. Our analysis indicated that positive outliers predominantly emerged in specific regions, including the right frontal operculum cortex, right inferior frontal gyrus (pars triangularis), and left anterior supramarginal  gyrus.  This  observation  aligned  with  former  findings, which also reported enhanced function connectivity in the frontal regions of MDD patients (Rolls et al., 2020). A higher degree centrality in a brain region suggests more instantaneous functional connections with other areas in the entire brain network (Liu and Lai, 2022; Zheng et al., 2023). It was reasonable to speculate that there was over-augmented integration  and  inter-regional  communication  in  the  above  brain  regions  during  MDD  conditions.  Previous  studies  have  highlighted  the underlying mechanisms by which alterations in degree centrality might impact emotion processing and attention control in MDD patients (Jia et al., 2023; Zhou et al., 2021). Given the crucial roles of these regions in managing  negative  emotions  and  directing  attention,  we  inferred, consistent with earlier findings, that abnormal increases in degree centrality within these regions might render MDD patients more susceptible to and focused on negative emotional stimuli (Higo et al., 2011; Laxton et al., 2013). Conversely, negative outliers were located across multiple regions, such as the left insula cortex, temporal lobe subregions, left inferior lateral occipital cortex, and sensorimotor cortices, which were involved in emotion processing, audiovisual information handling, and basic  sensory/motor  functions.  A  recent  study  has  reported  that decreased degree centrality among MDD patients revealed weakened functional  connectivity  in  brain  regions  associated  with  emotional regulation and cognitive impairments, and this weakening correlated with the severity and duration of the illness (Zheng et al., 2023). Our observations could be interpreted that the abnormal reduction in degree centrality  among  MDD  patients  may  reflect  neurobiological  changes
194:Table 2 Demographic and clinical differences between MDD subtypes.
226:associated with impaired emotional regulation and diminished capacity for sensory input processing. Importantly, we noticed a low overlap rate across  regions  presenting  positive  and  negative  outliers  among  MDD patients,  where  this  regional  heterogeneity  has  been  confirmed  in several past normative modeling researches (Shao et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2023). Our results not only emphasized the significant neuropathological feature of altered brain functions in MDD, but also highlighted the considerable variability in these alterations across individuals, which could lead to phenotypic heterogeneity (Segal et al., 2023) and provide potential clues for the diverse manifestations of MDD.

