68:In this study, we aimed to dissect the heterogeneity in MDD through normative model-driven subtyping of functional brain networks. Utilizing rs-fMRI data from a cohort of 797 MDD patients and 822 matched healthy  controls,  we  investigated  individual  deviations  in  functional brain network topology. By applying k-means clustering to deviation profiles derived from normative models of healthy brains, we sought to identify distinct MDD subtypes characterized by their unique functional brain network patterns. Moreover, we employed Neurosynth, a platform for large-scale automated synthesis of fMRI data (Yarkoni et al., 2011), to explore the cognitive correlates of these subtypes, gaining insights into how brain alterations may affect cognition. We hypothesized that MDD patients exhibited heterogeneity in the topological structure of functional brain networks, and there were potential MDD subtypes, each with  distinct  neurobiological  mechanisms,  clinical  symptoms,  and cognitive functions.
74:The sample data were derived from 1619 participants, encompassing 797  individuals  diagnosed  with  MDD  (mean  age:  35.167 ± 11.950 years, 289 males and 508 females) and 822 healthy controls (HC, mean age: 35.045 ± 13.336 years, 331 males and 491 females), see Fig. S1. These data were obtained from a publicly accessible dataset from the REST-meta-MDD project  (Yan et  al.,  2019)  provided  by  the  DIRECT consortium (Chen et al., 2022). Site 4 and Site 25 were excluded since Site 4's participants were duplicated with those from Site 14, and Site 25 contained an excessive number of late-onset depression cases (age &gt; 60 years) and remitted patients. We applied exclusion criteria for all participants as follows: 1) lack of information on sex, age, or education; 2) age &lt; 18 years or &gt; 65 years; 3) excessive head motion [mean framewise displacement (FD) &gt; 0.2 mm]. MDD patients were further excluded if their 17-item Hamilton Rating Scale for depression (HAMD) scores &lt; 8 points. We also removed participants with imaging distortions by visual inspection or with imaging signal lost in any region of interest (ROI) obtained from the cortical areas of the Harvard-Oxford atlas (Desikan et al., 2006; Kennedy et al., 1998). In addition, we did not include the sites with fewer than 10 MDD patients or 10 HC subjects to ensure that each site had enough MDD and HCs, thus balancing the objectives of optimizing the overall sample size and minimizing extreme biases. MDD diagnoses adhered to the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders,  Fourth  Edition  (DSM-IV)  or  International  Classification  of Diseases  10  (ICD-10)  criteria.  The  research  protocol  followed  the Declaration of Helsinki Ethical Principles and received approval from the local Institutional Review Boards (IRB) at each site. All participants provided the IRB-approved, written informed consent before engaging in the study procedures at their local institutions.
78:Brain image processing was performed according to a standardized protocol using the DPARSF software (http://www.rfmri.org/). The first ten functional volumes were discarded to allow for signal equilibration and environmental adaptation. The remaining data were corrected for the  time shifts  among different  acquisitions  within each  volume and then realigned to the first volume for head motion correction. The corrected  images  were  normalized  into  standard  Montreal  Neurological Institute space (resampling voxel size = 3 mm × 3 mm × 3 mm). The nuisance  regression  was  conducted  to  remove  Friston-24  motion  parameters, white matter signals, and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) signals. Temporal bandpass filtering was applied at a frequency range of 0.01 to 0.10 Hz.

